ve, corridor/pad/charger capacity, closures, and all intervention overrides. Optimize the stated task lexicographically: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy.\n\nNormative SOC event order: incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge. Post-service charge blocks attached to a mission's destination, after service flight and turnaround. Charger capacity applies to every occupied post-service charging slot at the destination. Check reserve immediately after incoming-deadhead plus service energy are deducted. Check battery upper bound after post-service charging.\n\nWe have baseline solution:\n- MISSION_01: UAV_03, path PATH_02, depart_slot 8, charge_blocks 0.\n- MISSION_02: UAV_03, path PATH_03, depart_slot 5, charge_blocks 0.\n- MISSION_03: UAV_01, path PATH_04, depart_slot 20, charge_blocks 0.\n- MISSION_04: UAV_02, path PATH_09, depart_slot 14, charge_blocks 0.\n- MISSION_05: UAV_02, path PATH_05, depart_slot 5, charge_blocks 0.\n- MISSION_06: UAV_01, path PATH_10, depart_slot 24, charge_blocks 0.\n- MISSION_07: UAV_03, path PATH_06, depart_slot 24, charge_blocks 0.\n\nThe baseline solution is for instance \"CA12_CORE_001\" with fixed UAV assignment and order. The missions are assigned to specific UAVs and in sequence order given by sequence_index. So we must keep the same UAV assignment and mission order. That means each UAV has a sequence of missions in the given order. For UAV_03: MISSION_02 (seq 0), MISSION_01 (seq 1), MISSION_07 (seq 2). For UAV_01: MISSION_03 (seq 0), MISSION_06 (seq 1). For UAV_02: MISSION_05 (seq 0), MISSION_04 (seq 1). We must respect that order. So depart slots must be non-decreasing with sequence index for each UAV.\n\nNow we have interventions: initial_soc_overrides for UAV_02: initial_soc_kwh 2.867135 (instead of 9.02689 from fleet). Also pad_capacity_overrides: \"capacity\":1 at VP_02 at slot 5. Closed corridors: COR_04 closed from slot 8 to 19, and COR_10 closed from slot 8 to 47 (it says slots 8-47, but note horizon_slots is 45; so effectively closed from slot 8 to 45). So COR_10 is closed from 8 onward (full horizon). COR_04 is closed from 8 to 19. Also if corridor closure is listed for slots beyond horizon, but horizon is 45, we consider up to 45. Slot numbers in interventions go up to 47, but horizon_slots 45, so up to 45. So COR_10 is closed for all slots from 8 to 45 inclusive. That means MISSION_01 can't use PATH_02 (COR_10) as it would depart at slot 8 and travel during slots 8-9, but COR_10 is closed at slot 8 and 9. PATH_02 uses COR_10, travel_slots 2, so it would occupy corridor at depart_slot and depart_slot+1. Since depart_slot must be >= release_slot=8, it would be at least 8. So PATH_02 is impossible because COR_10 closed from 8 onward. Also PATH_07 uses COR_11, which is not closed, but PATH_07 travel_slots=14. PATH_08 uses COR_04, travel_slots=2; COR_04 closed from 8-19, so if depart_slot is 8, it would use slot 8-9 which are closed. So MISSION_01 cannot use any path? Let's check: MISSION_01 origin VP_05, destination VP_03. Path options: PATH_02 (COR_10, closed 8+), PATH_07 (COR_11, not closed, 14 slots, distance 7.68 km), PATH_08 (COR_04, closed 8-19, 2 slots). So only PATH_07 is available if we depart at slot 8 or later, because COR_10 and COR_04 are closed at slot 8. PATH_07 is 14 slots, distance 7.68 km, energy 0.86784 kWh. That's long. It may be possible if energy and timing allow.\n\nMISSION_02: UAV_03, seq 0, origin VP_02 -> VP_05, path PATH_03 (COR_09, 2 slots, distance 1.05, energy 0.11865). Release slot 5. The baseline had depart_slot 5. COR_09 is not closed. So that seems fine.\n\nMISSION_07: UAV_03 seq 2, origin VP_03 -> VP_07, path PATH_06 (COR_07, 1 slot, distance 7.68, energy 0.963302). Release slot 24. No closures there.\n\nMISSION_03: UAV_01 seq 0, origin VP_02 -> VP_06, path PATH_04 (COR_06, 2 slots, wind level 1 energy 0.160815). Release 20. No