.992474. Turnaround slot 22 (no change). Post-service charge blocks for M01: c=1. The charge block occupies slot 23? According to formula: after turnaround at slot 22, charge block slot = s+t+1 = 8+14+1 = 23. At the end of charge block, SOC increases by 1.2 kWh, but capped at capacity 16. So after charge block, SOC = min(1.992474 + 1.2, 16) = 3.192474. Then UAV ready at slot 24. So at departure for M07 (slot 24), SOC = 3.192474. Reserve check: need 1.5 + 0.963302 = 2.463302, OK. After M07 flight (slots 24, travel 1, ends at end of slot 24?), consumption 0.963302, SOC = 3.192474 - 0.963302 = 2.229172. Turnaround slot 25 (no change). Post-service charge: c=0. So final SOC 2.229172, below reserve? No, reserve only checked before flights. OK.\n\nNow check charger capacity at VP_03 during slot 23: charger_capacity_default 2, only this charge block, so fine. Also pad capacity: VP_03 pad capacity 2.\n\nCheck release/due: M02 due 7, depart 5 -> arrival end of 6? due slot is when mission should be completed? The due_slot is 7. M02 arrival at 6, turnaround at 7, but the mission is considered complete after turnaround? Or after flight? Typically due_slot is the latest slot by which the mission must be completed. \"Due slot\" might mean the mission must be finished (including turnaround?) by due_slot. Baseline M02 depart 5, travel 2, due 7. It finishes travel at 6, turnaround 7, so it's complete by slot 7. So due_slot likely includes turnaround. So M01 due 25, with depart 8, travel 14, turnaround 22? Actually arrival at 21, turnaround 22, so it completes at slot 22? That's before due 25. M07 due 28, depart 24, travel 1, turnaround 25, so completes 25, before due. So timing OK.\n\nNow UAV_02: initial SOC override 2.867135, battery cap 22, reserve 2.5, charge/block 0.95. Missions: M05->M04. M05 seq 0, origin VP_02->VP_05, path options: PATH_01 (3 slots, 0.302 kWh), PATH_05 (7 slots, 5.403384 kWh), PATH_11 (2 slots, 0.308765 kWh). All wind levels ok. M05 release 5, due 11. M04 seq 1, origin VP_05->VP_04, path PATH_09 (1 slot, 0.123506 kWh), release 14, due 23.\n\nWe need to choose path for M05. If we use PATH_05 (energy 5.403384), that will consume 5.403384 from initial 2.867135, which is impossible (would be negative). So PATH_05 is infeasible unless we charge before. But M05 is first mission, no charging possible before it (initial SOC is given). So we need initial SOC >= reserve + energy. For PATH_05, need 2.5+5.403384=7.903384 > 2.867135, so infeasible. PATH_01 needs 2.5+0.302=2.802, which is slightly less than 2.867135, so feasible: SOC before M05 2.867135 >= 2.802. After M05, SOC = 2.867135 - 0.302 = 2.565135. PATH_11 needs 2.5+0.308765=2.808765, also feasible (2.867135 > 2.808765). So we can choose PATH_01 or PATH_11. Baseline used PATH_05, but that's now infeasible due to the initial SOC override (likely the intervention reduced it). So we must change path. PATH_11 is faster (2 slots vs 3) and only slightly more energy (0.308765 vs 0.302). But PATH_11 has wind level 1, which UAV_02 max wind 2, ok. PATH_01 wind 0. So both are compatible. Lexicographic objective: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy. We want to optimize, but first minimize holds (we don't want to hold missions unless necessary). Here, M05 can be served without hold. So we need to pick path and charge_blocks for M05 and M04 to minimize distance, tardiness, etc. Distance: PATH_01 2.0 km, PATH_11 1.92 km. So PATH_11 has shorter distance. So we should prefer PATH_11 for repair distance (lower distance better). Tardiness: M05 due 11. If depart 5, PATH_01 travel 3, turnaround 1, completes at 5+3+1=9, no tardiness. PATH_11 travel 2, turnaround 1, completes at 5+2+1=8, also no tardiness. So both ok. So we'll pick PATH_11 for shorter distance. Then M04: origin VP_05 -> VP_04, PATH_09 (1 slot, energy 0.123506). M04 release 14. After M05 ends at VP_05. M05 with PATH_11: depart 5, arrival 5+2-1=6? travel 2 slots, so arrival slot 6? complete at 8? But we need departure for M04 earliest. With M05 depart 5, travel 2, turnaround 1, c=0, ready at 5+2+1=8. M04 release 14, so we can depart at 14 or later. So we have a gap. We could potentially hold M05? No, minimizing holds. So depart M05 at 5, then wait. Could we delay M05? release 5, so can't be earlier. Could depart at 5 is ok. Then M04 depart >= 14. We can choose depart_slot for M04 as 14. Then compute energy: After M05 (no charge), SOC = 2.867135 - 0.308765 = 2.55837. Reserve check before M04: need 2.5 + 0.123506 = 2.623506. Current 2.55837 < 2.623506, so insufficient! So we need to charge after M05 to have enough energy for M04. M05 post-service charge blocks could be added at VP_05 after its turnaround. M05 completes turnaround at 5+2+1=8. Then we can add c charge blocks. Each block adds 0.95 kWh. To get SOC from 2.55837 to at least 2.623506, we need at least 0.065136 kWh, so one block is enough (0.95). So c=1. That will add 0.95 kWh. Then SOC = 2.55837 + 0.95 = 3.50837 (cap 22, ok). Then M04 depart at 14, SOC 3.50837, reserve check ok. After M04, SOC = 3.50837 - 0.123506 = 3.384864. Then turnaround, c=0. So that works. We need to check charger capacity at VP_05 during charge block slot. M05 charge block will be at slot 8? Since s=5, t=2, turnaround 1 -> charge block slot = s + t + 1 = 5+2+1=8. So slot 8. Charger capacity VP_05 default 2. Check if any other charging at VP_05 at slot 8. UAV_03 M02 ends at VP_05, turnaround at slot 7? M02 depart 5, travel 2, turnaround 1, so charge block slot would be 5+2+1=8, but we set charge_blocks=0 for M02. So no charging from UAV_03. So charger available. Also pad capacity VP_05 is 2 default, no override. So fine. So UAV_02: M05 PATH_11 depart 5, charge_blocks=1; M04 PATH_09 depart 14, charge_blocks=0. Check due: M05 due 11, completes at 5+2+1=8, ok. M04 due 23, complet at 14+1+1=16, ok. So this seems feasible.\n\nNow UAV_01: initial SOC 8.5, reserve 2.5, cap 22, charge/block 0.95. Missions: M03 (seq 0) VP_02->VP_06, PATH_04 (2 slots, 0.160815), release 20, due 27. M06 (seq 1) VP_06->VP_01, PATH_10 (1 slot, 0.10872), release 24, due 30. Baseline: M03 depart 20, c=0; M06 depart 24, c=0. That's fine. We need to check closures: no closures affecting these corridors. Also check initial SOC and energy: M03 need 2.5+0.160815=2.660815, ok. After M03: SOC=8.5-0.160815=8.339185. Turnaround at VP_06, slot 20+2+1=23 ready. M06 depart 24, no charge needed, SOC=8.339185 > 2.60872, ok. After M06, SOC=8.230465. So UAV_01 works as baseline. Could we improve distance? M03 path only PATH_04, so no alternative. M06 only PATH_10. So no change needed. So keep depart 20, 24, charge 0 each.\n\nNow check all corridor capacities: The only potential congestion is COR_09 (M02), COR_11 (M01), COR_02? Actually M05 uses PATH_11 with COR_03. COR_03: no closure. COR_06 (M03), COR_01 (M06). All capacities default 2. Only one mission using each corridor in our schedule, so no conflict. Also pad capacities: VP_02 at slot 5: override pad capacity 1 at slot 5. Our missions: M02 UAV_03 depart VP_02 at slot 5, M05 UAV_02 depart VP_02 at slot 5. Both depart from VP_02 at slot 5. VP_02 pad_capacity default 2, but override at slot 5 capacity=1. So only one UAV can be at VP_02 at slot 5. That's a conflict! We have both M02 and M05 departing at the same slot 5 from VP_02. That would exceed pad capacity of 1 at slot 5. So we must adjust. Either delay one departure, or hold one mission? But we need to resolve. The fixed UAV order and release slots: M02 release 5, M05 release 5. Both can depart at 5. But pad capacity at VP_02 at slot 5 is 1. So one of them must delay departure to slot 6 or later. However, release slot is 5, so they can depart later than release, but that might cause tardiness. So we need to choose which one to delay. We want to minimize weighted tardiness, delay, holds, etc. Lets evaluate consequences.\n\nUAV_03 M02 due 7. If we delay departure to 6, travel 2, arrival 7, turnaround 8, then M01 depart at 8 earliest? M01 release 8, due 25. With M02 depart 6, travel 2, turnaround 1, ready for M01 at 6+2+1=9. So M01 depart would be at least 9. But M01 due 25, that's fine. However, M01 travel 14 slots, so if depart 9, arrival 23, turnaround 24, then with c=1, ready at 25. M07 release 24, due 28. If M01 departs 9, with c=1, ready at 9+14+1+1=25? Actually earliest_next = depart + travel + 1 + c? Let's use formula: earliest next departure = s + t + 1 + c. For M01: s=9, t=14, c=1 -> ready at 9+14+1+1=25. So M07 would depart at 25. That's after release 24, and still before due 28. So that works. Energy: M02 delayed doesn't change energy. So possible. But tardiness: M02 due 7. If it departs 5, completes at 8, tardy? Completion: depart 5, travel 2, turnaround 1 -> complete at 8? Wait, we don't know if due_slot includes turnaround. In baseline M02 due 7, depart 5, travel 2, turnaround 1 => completion at slot 8? But baseline considered that ok. So due 7 likely means the mission must be completed (including turnaround) by slot 7? But baseline had M02 depart 5, travel 2, which would finish flight at 6, turnaround at 7, so completion at 7? I think due_slot is the latest slot by which the mission must be finished, including turnaround? The baseline had M02 due 7, depart 5, travel 2. If flight ends at 6, turnaround at 7, then it finishes at 7, right at due. So if we delay M02 departure to 6, then flight would end at 7, turnaround at 8, completion at 8 > due 7, so tardy. Tardiness would be 1 slot. Weighted tardiness: we don't have explicit weights, but lexicographic: after holds, we have repair distance, then weighted tardiness, then delay+charge wait. There is no weight given, so presumably all tasks equal weight. So we need to avoid tardiness if possible. So delaying M02 would cause tardiness. Instead, we could delay M05 departure to 6. M05 due 11, if depart 6, with PATH_11 travel 2, turnaround 1, completion at 6+2+1=9, still before due 11. So no tardiness. So it's better to delay M05 to slot 6, and keep M02 at slot 5. That respects pad capacity at VP_02 slot 5: only M02 at slot 5. M05 at slot 6. VP_02 pad capacity for slot 6? No override, so cap 2. So ok.\n\nCheck M05 departure at slot 6: release 5, so allowed. Then M04 earliest depart = 6+2+1=9. But M04 release 14, so we can depart at 14. That's fine. SOC after M05 and c=1: we will have charge block at slot? If M05 depart 6, travel 2, turnaround 1, charge block slots = 6+2+1=9? Actually s+t+1 = 6+2+1=9. So charge block at slot 9. Then ready at 10. So M04 can depart at 14, ok. SOC calculations: initial UAV_02 SOC 2.867135. After M05 at depart 6, no change until flight. Flight consumes 0.308765, so at end of flight (slot 7) SOC = 2.867135 - 0.308765 = 2.55837. Turnaround slot 8. Charge block slot 9 adds 0.95 -> SOC = 3.50837. Then idle until depart M04 at 14. So SOC 3.50837, reserve check ok. So this works.\n\nNow verify charger capacity at VP_05 slot 9: UAV_02 charge block. No other missions. So fine.\n\nNow check all corridor closures again: M01 using PATH_07 (COR_11) depart 8? But with M02 depart 5, M01 depart 8 is fine. COR_11 not closed. So all good.\n\nNow, could we improve further? Maybe choose PATH_01 for M05? PATH_01 distance 2.0 > 1.92, so PATH_11 is shorter distance. So that's optimal for repair distance. We could also consider holding M05 or something? Holding means \"held\" = true, which would skip mission entirely? But we must include every mission exactly once, but held missions are counted as holds. We want to minimize holds. So no holds. So our decisions: M02 depart 5 PATH_03 c=0; M01 depart 8 PATH_07 c=1; M07 depart 24 PATH_06 c=0 for UAV_03. For UAV_02: M05 depart 6 PATH_11 c=1; M04 depart 14 PATH_09 c=0. For UAV_01: M03 depart 20 PATH_04 c=0; M06 depart 24 PATH_10 c=0. That seems feasible.\n\nWe must check all constraints: \n- Fixed UAV assignment and order respected.\n- Corridor capacity: All corridors used once per slot: COR_09 (M02), COR_11 (M01 14 slots 8-21), COR_07 (M07 1 slot 24), COR_03 (M05 2 slots 6-7), COR_05 (M04 1 slot 14), COR_06 (M03 2 slots 20-21), COR_01 (M06 1 slot 24). No overlaps. Capacities 2, so fine.\n- Pad capacities: VP_02 slot 5: M02 depart, only 1 (since M05 moved to slot 6). VP_02 slot 6: M05 depart, no other. VP_05 slot 5? No departures. VP_05 slot 8? M01 depart from VP_05 at 8? M01 origin VP_05, depart 8. That's pad usage at VP_05 slot 8. VP_05 pad capacity 2, ok. VP_05 slot 6? No. VP_03 slot 24 M07 depart, etc. All fine.\n- Charger capacities: M01 charge at VP_03 slot 23 (s=8, t=14, so charge slot = 8+14+1=23). No other charging. M05 charge at VP_05 slot 9. Fine.\n- Energy calculations checked.\n- Turnaround: 1 slot each.\n- Release/due: M02 depart 5 >= 5, due 7: completion: depart 5, travel 2, turnaround 1 -> completion slot = depart+travel+turnaround? Actually when is mission complete? I'll assume complete when turnaround ends, i.e., at slot s+t+turnaround - 1? With s=5, t=2, turnaround 1, completion at slot 5+2+1-1=7? Let's be careful: In baseline, M02 due 7, depart 5, travel 2, turnaround 1, and it was considered not tardy. So completion slot is the end of turnaround, which is at s+t+turnaround - 1? Since turnaround occupies slots starting at s+t. If turnaround=1, it occupies slot s+t exactly. So completion at slot s+t. So if s=5, t=2, turnaround occupies slot 7, completion at 7. So due slot 7 means it must be completed at or before slot 7. That matches. So for M02, completion = 7, due 7, ok.\nM05: depart 6, t=2, turnover=1, completion = 6+2=8? Wait, s=6, t=2, turnaround slot = 6+2=8, completion at 8. Due 11, ok.\nM01: depart 8, t=14, turnaround=1, completion = 8+14=22, due 25, ok.\nM07: depart 24, t=1, turnaround=1, completion = 24+1=25, due 28, ok.\nM03: depart 20, t=2, turnaround=1, completion = 20+2=22, due 27, ok.\nM06: depart 24, t=1, turnaround=1, completion = 24+1=25, due 30, ok.\n\nAll within due.\n\nNow check if we can reduce distance further? M01 PATH_07 distance 7.68 km, but we had no other feasible path due to closures. Could we use PATH_08 with depart later to avoid COR_04 closure? COR_04 closed slots 8-19. PATH_08 uses COR_04, travel 2 slots. To avoid closure, we could depart at slot 20 or later (so that travel slots 20-21 are after closure ends at 19). But M01 release 8, due 25. If we depart at 20, travel 2, turnaround 1, completion at 22, due 25 ok. Then M07 would depart earliest at 20+2+1=23. M07 release 24, so could depart 24. That's still okay. But we would need to check energy for M01 with PATH_08: energy 0.120413 (wind 1). That is much lower than 0.86784. So better for energy consumption. Also repair distance: PATH_08 distance 0.96 km vs PATH_07 7.68 km, so much better for repair distance objective. And PATH_08 is compatible (UAV_03 max wind level 1, wind level 1 ok). So this is a better solution! But we must check if delaying M01 to 20 affects M02-M01 gap. UAV_03 after M02 completes at 7 (s=5,t=2, turnaround 7). Then UAV is idle at VP_05 from slot 7 onward. M01 depart 20, that's a long wait, but allowed. M07 depart 24, ok. So this reduces distance and energy, and also may reduce charge wait? We only have one charge block after M01? We might need to check energy. With M01 PATH_08 energy 0.120413, we could potentially avoid charging entirely. Let's calculate.\n\nUAV_03: initial SOC 2.978964. M02: energy 0.11865, after M02 SOC = 2.860314. M01 PATH_08 energy 0.120413, need reserve 1.5+0.120413=1.620413, current 2.860314 ok. After M01, SOC = 2.860314 - 0.120413 = 2.739901. M07: need 1.5+0.963302=2.463302, current 2.739901 ok. So no charge needed! So charge_blocks=0 for M01. Then M01 depart 20, travel 2, turnaround 1, completion 20+2=22, ready at 23. M07 depart 24, ok. That avoids charging altogether, reducing charge wait. So this is a much better solution: lower distance (1.05? actually total distance is sum of path distances). So we should adopt this.\n\nThus UAV_03: M02: depart 5 PATH_03 c=0; M01: depart 20 PATH_08 c=0; M07: depart 24 PATH_06 c=0. Check if M01 depart 20 violates any closure? PATH_08 uses COR_04, which is closed 8-19. Depart 20 means travel slots 20-21, which are after closure ends at 19, so ok. Check due: M01 due 25, completion at 20+2=22, ok. So feasible.\n\nNow check pad capacity at VP_05 for M01 depart 20: okay. No other departures at VP_05 at slot 20? UAV_02 M05 at VP_05? M05 arrival at VP_05 at slot? M05 depart 6, travel 2, arrival at 7, turnaround 8, charge block 9, then idle until M04 depart 14 from VP_05. So M04 depart is at 14, so at slot 20, VP_05 pad is free. So ok. Also M01 arrival at VP_03 at slot? 20+2=22, turnaround 23, then M07 depart 24, pad ok.\n\nNow, UAV_02: M05 with PATH_11 depart 6 c=1; M04 depart 14 c=0. We could adjust M05 path to PATH_01? That would increase distance (2.0 > 1.92), so not better. Could we reduce charge blocks? We needed 1 charge block to get enough SOC for M04. Could we delay M04 to allow more idle and maybe use initial SOC without charging? Without charge, SOC after M05 would be 2.55837, need 2.623506, so insufficient. So must charge or hold M04? Holding is not allowed (we don't want holds). So charge required. So that is good.\n\nNow UAV_01 stays same.\n\nNow let's verify energy for UAV_02 with M05 path PATH_11 depart 6, c=1: SOC initial 2.867135. M05 flight energy 0.308765, after flight SOC = 2.55837. Then turnaround (slot 8), charge block slot 9 adds 0.95 -> SOC = 3.50837. Idle until M04 depart 14. So SOC 3.50837 >= 2.623506, ok. After M04: SOC = 3.50837 - 0.123506 = 3.384864. Reserve check after M04 not needed, but next mission none. So fine.\n\nNow check total repair distance: Sum of path distances: M02 (PATH_03) 1.05 km; M01 (PATH_08) 0.96 km; M07 (PATH_06) 7.68 km; M05 (PATH_11) 1.92 km; M04 (PATH_09) 0.768 km; M03 (PATH_04) 1.0 km; M06 (PATH_10) 0.72 km. Total = 1.05+0.96+7.68+1.92+0.768+1.0+0.72 = 14.098 km. Baseline had: M01 0.96 (PATH_02) but closed; M02 1.05; M03 1.0; M04 0.768; M05 33.6 (PATH_05); M06 0.72; M07 7.68. Sum = 0.96+1.05+1.0+0.768+33.6+0.72+7.68 = 45.778 km. So huge improvement.\n\nNow check if we can reduce M01's distance further? The only paths are PATH_02 (closed), PATH_07 (7.68), PATH_08 (0.96). We chose PATH_08. That's the shortest available after closure window. So good.\n\nCheck UAV_03 M07 path: only PATH_06 (7.68 km). So can't reduce.\n\nUAV_02 M04 path: only PATH_09.\n\nUAV_02 M05 path: PATH_01 (2.0) vs PATH_11 (1.92). We chose 1.92. So optimal.\n\nUAV_01 tasks no alternatives.\n\nNow ensure all interventions considered: pad_capacity VP_02 slot 5=1 respected. So we delayed M05 to 6, and M02 at 5. So only one depart at VP_02 slot 5. Good.\n\nAlso initial SOC override for UAV_02 used.\n\nCorridor closures: COR_04 closed 8-19, we avoided by departing M01 at 20. COR_10 closed 8-45, we didn't use it.\n\nAll good.\n\nNow produce JSON. The decisions array must have all missions exactly once. The order doesn't matter. We'll list all 7 missions with our decisions.\n\n- MISSION_01: uav_id UAV_03, path_id PATH_08, depart_slot 20, charge_blocks 0, held false.\n- MISSION_02: uav_id UAV_03, path_id PATH_03, depart_slot 5, charge_blocks 0, held false.\n- MISSION_03: uav_id UAV_01, path_id PATH_04, depart_slot 20, charge_blocks 0, held false.\n- MISSION_04: uav_id UAV_02, path_id PATH_09, depart_slot 14, charge_blocks 0, held false.\n- MISSION_05: uav_id UAV_02, path_id PATH_11, depart_slot 6, charge_blocks 1, held false.\n- MISSION_06: uav_id UAV_01, path_id PATH_10, depart_slot 24, charge_blocks 0, held false.\n- MISSION_07: uav_id UAV_03, path_id PATH_06, depart_slot 24, charge_blocks 0, held false.\n\nCheck depart_slot and charge_blocks: M05 charge_blocks=1. All others 0.\n\nNow double-check M05 charge block timing: M05 depart 6, travel 2, turnaround 1 -> charge block slot = 6+2+1 = 9. So charger VP_05 slot 9 used. Charge block count 1. That's fine.\n\nNow verify SOC events order for each:\n\nUAV_03:\n- M02: before: SOC 2.978964, incoming deadhead none. Service 